In article <51c14c0@donor2.demon.co.uk>, alanc@donor2.demon.co.uk says...
>
>Hello all
>
>I have a dialog set up in an OPL program.  The ESC key completes the 
dialog as
>it should with zero and the enter key returns the line number. However, 
the
>HELP key also completes the dialog and, unfortunately, also returns zero. 
 Is
>it possible to either, prevent the help key from completing the dialog 
or,
>better still, detect whether the ESC key or the HELP key completed the 
dialog?
>
>Alan
>
Try the following code which will only work on a 3a (i.e. not the older 
Series 3):

#define O_GT_LAST_KEY  23

/*  This method gets the last key processed by the system
    on behalf of the application  */


PROC main:

        LOCAL   gate%  /*  Handle of the gate object  */
        LOCAL   c%  /*  Live variable fo rthe dCHOICE  */
        LOCAL   k%  /*  Variable to receive the key  */
        LOCAL   mods%  /*  Variable to receive modifiers  */
        LOCAL   pmods%  /*  Pointer to above  */

        dINIT "Test dialog"
        dCHOICE c%, "Choice", "No, Yes, Maybe"
        DIALOG  /*  Run the dialog  */

        gate% = PEEKW($38)  /*  Find the handle of the GATE object  */
        pmods% = ADDR(mods%)  /*  Point pmods% to the right place  */
        k% = ENTERSEND(gate%, O_GT_LAST_KEY, #pmods%)  /*  Send the 
message  */

        PRINT "Key was   =", k%
        PRINT "Mods were =", mods%
        GET

ENDP

The comments should be straightforward. The only other thing you need to 
know is that the modifiers written to 'pmods%' will be:

2 for <Shift>
4 for <Control>
8 for <Psion>

and these can be combined (i.e. <Psion><Shift><Help> will return 291 in k% 
and 10 in mods%)

Hope this helps

Ali


